**user**:
> How do I match in regex either a word boundary or the end of the string?
**assistant**:
> Use alternation:
> 
> ```regex
> (?:\b|$)
> ```
> 
> For example:
> 
> ```regex
> foo(?:\b|$)
> ```
> 
> matches `foo` when followed by a word boundary or the string end.
> 
> If you specifically need the *absolute* end of the whole string (not line end in multiline mode), use your language’s strict end anchor—for example, Python:
> 
> ```regex
> foo(?:\b|\Z)
> ```
> 
> Often `\b` already matches at the end of a string when the preceding character is a word character, so the explicit end alternative mainly matters when the preceding character is non-word.
